<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Without calculating, decide whether each estimate is an overestimate, an underestimate or the exact area.</p><p>First decide whether each curve bends upwards, bends downwards or is a straight line on its interval.</p><ol><li><div>The trapezoidal rule with <m>4</m> strips for <m>y = {-x^2+2 x+8}</m> from <m>x = {0}</m> to <m>x = {3}</m> gives [ an overestimate | an underestimate | the exact area ] </div></li><li><div>The trapezoidal rule with <m>4</m> strips for <m>y = {x^3+1}</m> from <m>x = {0}</m> to <m>x = {2}</m> gives [ an overestimate | an underestimate | the exact area ] </div></li><li><div>The mid-ordinate rule with <m>4</m> strips for <m>y = {5-x}</m> from <m>x = {0}</m> to <m>x = {3}</m> gives [ an overestimate | an underestimate | the exact area ] </div></li><li><div>The mid-ordinate rule with <m>4</m> strips for <m>y = {2 x^2+x+4}</m> from <m>x = {0}</m> to <m>x = {2}</m> gives [ an overestimate | an underestimate | the exact area ] </div></li><li><div>The mid-ordinate rule with <m>4</m> strips for <m>y = {-x^2+x+6}</m> from <m>x = {0}</m> to <m>x = {2}</m> gives [ an overestimate | an underestimate | the exact area ] </div></li></ol><div/></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>For a curve that bends upwards the chords lie above the curve, so the trapezoidal rule overestimates; the mid-ordinate rule errs the other way and underestimates. For a curve that bends downwards both are reversed. For a straight line both rules give the exact area. The second derivative tells the bend: positive on the interval means it bends upwards.</p><ol><li><p><m>y = {-x^2+2 x+8}</m>, <m>{0} \le x \le {3}</m>: <m>\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {-2}</m>, never positive on this interval, so the curve bends downwards. The trapezoidal rule gives an underestimate.</p></li><li><p><m>y = {x^3+1}</m>, <m>{0} \le x \le {2}</m>: <m>\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {6 x}</m>, never negative on this interval, so the curve bends upwards. The trapezoidal rule gives an overestimate.</p></li><li><p><m>y = {5-x}</m>, <m>{0} \le x \le {3}</m>: <m>\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {0}</m>, so the graph is a straight line. The mid-ordinate rule gives the exact area.</p></li><li><p><m>y = {2 x^2+x+4}</m>, <m>{0} \le x \le {2}</m>: <m>\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {4}</m>, never negative on this interval, so the curve bends upwards. The mid-ordinate rule gives an underestimate.</p></li><li><p><m>y = {-x^2+x+6}</m>, <m>{0} \le x \le {2}</m>: <m>\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {-2}</m>, never positive on this interval, so the curve bends downwards. The mid-ordinate rule gives an overestimate.</p></li></ol></p></solution>
    <answer><p>an underestimate</p></answer>
    <answer><p>an overestimate</p></answer>
    <answer><p>the exact area</p></answer>
    <answer><p>an underestimate</p></answer>
    <answer><p>an overestimate</p></answer>
</stack-static>